<article class="env-lemma" id="0H0W">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0H0W">Lemma <span data-tag="0H0W">85.8.5</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/09WE" data-tag="09WE">85.3.3</a>. Let $K$ be an object of $D(\mathcal{C}_{total})$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $H^{-p}(\mathcal{C}_ p, K_ p) = 0$ for all $p \geq 0$, then $H^0(\mathcal{C}_{total}, K) = 0$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $R\Gamma (\mathcal{C}_ p, K_ p) = 0$ for all $p \geq 0$, then $R\Gamma (\mathcal{C}_{total}, K) = 0$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      With notation as in Remark <a href="/tag/0H0V" data-tag="0H0V">85.8.4</a> we see that $R\Gamma (\mathcal{C}_{total}, K)$ is represented by $\text{Tot}_\pi (A^{\bullet , \bullet })$. The assumption in (1) tells us that $H^{-p}(A^{p, \bullet }) = 0$. Thus the vanishing in (1) follows from More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0H0Q" data-tag="0H0Q">15.105.1</a>. Part (2) follows from part (1) and taking shifts. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>